readings.c1s1:
¶
{{smaller block|It enabled him to gain the most vain and impracticable into his designs, and to ''bring about'' several great events for the advantage of the public. {{right|''Addison'', Freeholder.}}}}
{{smaller block|Yes, yes, ’faith, they’re agreed—he’s caught, he’s entangled—my dear Carlos, we have ''brought'' it ''about.''
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Duenna, ii. 4.}}}}
'''To bring a chain cable to''', to put it round the capstan ready for heaving up the anchor.—'''To bring a nest of hornets about one’s ears.''' See ''hornet.''—'''To bring a person to his bearings.''' See ''bearing.''—'''To bring a ship to anchor''', to let go the anchor.—'''To bring by the lee''' (''naut.''), to have the wind come suddenly on the lee side, owing to the yawing of the vessel, a sudden change in the wind’s direction, or the bad steering of the helmsman.—'''To bring down.''' (''a'') To take down; cause to come down; lower. (''b'') To humiliate; abase. ''Shak.'' (''c'') To cause to fall; hence, of game, to kill. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|By my valour! there is no merit in killing him so near: do, my dear Sir Lucius, let me ''bring'' him ''down'' at a long shot. {{right|''Sheridan'', The Rivals, v. 3.}}}}
'''To bring down the house''', to elicit a burst of applause or laughter from those present, as in acting or public speaking.—'''To bring far ben.''' See ''ben''<sup>1</sup>.—'''To bring forth.''' (''a'') To produce, as young or fruit; hence, give rise to; be the cause of.
{{smaller block|Idleness and luxury ''bring forth'' poverty and want.
{{right|''Tillotson.''}}}}
(''b'') To bring to light; disclose; reveal.
{{smaller block|<poem>The heavens have thought well on thee, . .
To ''bring forth'' this discovery.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', All’s Well, v. 3.}}}}
'''To bring forward.''' (''a'') To produce to view; cause to advance. (''b'') To adduce: as, to ''bring forward'' arguments in support of a scheme.—'''To bring grist to the mill.''' See ''grist.''—'''To bring home to.''' (''a'') To prove conclusively to belong or be applicable to or be true of, as a charge of any kind. (''b'') To impress upon the feeling; cause to be felt: as, he ''brought'' it ''home to'' them very vividly; in preaching, strive to ''bring'' the truth ''home to'' the hearers.

readings.c1s2:
¶
{{smaller block|Several prisoners ''to'' whom Jeffreys was unable to ''bring home'' the charge of high treason were convicted of misdemeanours. {{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng.}}}}

'''To bring in.''' (''a'') To bring from another place, or from without to within a certain precinct.
{{smaller block|Look you ''bring'' me ''in'' the names of some six or seven.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., ii. 1.}}}}
(''b'') To supply; furnish; yield: especially used in speaking of a revenue, rent, or income produced from a certain source.
{{smaller block|The sole measure of all his courtesies is, what return they will make him, and what revenue they will ''bring'' him ''in.'' {{right|''South.''}}}}
(''c'') To introduce; especially, to introduce to the notice of a legislature: as, to ''bring in'' a bill.
{{smaller block|Cain was not therefore the first murderer, but Adam, who ''brought in'' death. ''Sir T. Browne'', Religio Medici, ii. 4.

Since he could not have a seat among them himself, he would ''bring in'' one who had more merit. {{right|''Tatler.''}}}}
(''d'') To place in a particular condition or station.
{{smaller block|<poem>But he protests he loves you;
And needs no other suitor but his likings . . .
To ''bring'' you ''in'' again [namely, to your former office].</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, iii. 1.}}}}
(''e''†) To reduce within the limits of law and government.
{{smaller block|Perforce ''bring in'' all that rebellious rout.
{{right|''Spenser'', State of Ireland.}}}}

'''To bring off.''' (''a'') To bear or convey from a place; rescue: as, to ''bring off'' men from a wreck.
{{smaller block|<poem>A brave young fellow, of a matchless spirit!
He ''brought'' me ''off'' like thunder, charg’d and boarded,
As if he had been shot to save mine honour.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Knight of Malta, ii. 1.}}}}
(''b'') To procure to be acquitted; clear from condemnation; cause to escape. (''c'') To dissuade; change, as from an opinion or purpose; cause to abandon.

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|’Tis a foolish thing for me to be ''brought off'' from an Opinion in a thing neither of us know.
{{right|''Selden'', Table-Talk, p. 79.}}}}
'''To bring on.''' (''a'') To bear or convey or cause to be conveyed with one from a distance: as, to ''bring on'' a quantity of goods. (''b'') To cause to begin: as, to ''bring on'' a battle.
{{smaller block|All commanders were cautioned against ''bringing on'' an engagement. {{right|''U. S. Grant'', Personal Memoirs, I. 373.}}}}
(''c'') To originate or cause to exist: as, to ''bring on'' a disease. (''d'') To induce; lead on.
{{smaller block|<poem>With a crafty madness, keeps aloof,
When we would ''bring'' him ''on'' to some confession.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, iii. 1.}}}}
'''To bring one’s nose to the grindstone.''' See ''grindstone.''—'''To bring out.''' (''a'') To expose; detect; bring to light from concealment: as, to ''bring out'' one’s baseness. (''b'') To find by calculation or argument; deduce; infer.
{{smaller block|The more strictly Mr. Gladstone reasons on his premises, the more absurd are the conclusions which he ''brings out.''
{{right|''Macaulay'', Gladstone on Church and State.}}}}
(''c'') To publish: as, to ''bring out'' a new edition of a book.—'''To bring over.''' (''a'') To carry over; bear across: as, to ''bring over'' despatches; to ''bring over'' passengers in a boat. (''b'') To convert by persuasion or other means; draw to a new party; cause to change sides or an opinion.
{{smaller block|What did I not undergo of danger in this negotiation to have ''brought'' him ''over'' to his Majesty’s interest, when it was intirely in his hands! ''Evelyn'', Diary, May 24, 1660.

The Protestant clergy will find it perhaps no difficult matter to ''bring'' great numbers ''over'' to the church. ''Swift.''}}
'''To bring round.''' (''a'') To persuade: as, I will undertake to ''bring'' him ''round'' to your views. (''b'') To lead up to in an indirect manner: as, he ''brought round'' the conversation to his favorite topic. (''c'') To recover, as from a swoon.—'''To bring to.''' (''a'') To bring back to consciousness as a person partly drowned. (''b'') ''Naut.'': (1) To heave to; force (another ship) to heave to or stop. (2) To bend

readings.c2s1:
(a sail) to its yard or gaff.—'''To bring to bag,''' in ''hunting,'' to kill.—'''To bring to bear,''' or '''to bear upon.''' (''a'') To cause to have influence or effect, or to operate upon.
{{smaller block|Every author has a way of his own in ''bringing'' his points ''to bear.''
{{right|''Sterne'', Tristram Shandy, i. 9.}}}}
{{smaller block|All powerful action is performed by ''bringing'' the forces of nature ''to bear upon'' our objects.
{{right|''Emerson'', Art.}}}}
{{smaller block|No force of imagination that I can ''bring to bear'' will avail to cast out the youth of that very imagination which endeavours to depict its latter days.
{{right|''W. K. Clifford'', Lectures, I. 230.}}}}
(''b'') To bring into range, or the range of: as, to ''bring'' a gun ''to bear upon'' a target.—'''To bring to book.''' See ''book.''—'''To bring to gaff.''' See ''gaff.''—'''To bring to light,''' to bring into view; reveal.—'''To bring to mind,''' to recall, as what has been forgotten or what is not present to the mind.—'''To bring to pass,''' to cause to come to pass; effect.
{{smaller block|The thing is established by God, and God will shortly ''bring'' it ''to pass.''
{{right|Gen. xli. 32.}}}}
'''To bring to the gangway.''' See ''gangway.''—'''To bring to the hammer.''' See ''hammer.''—'''To bring under,''' to subdue; repress; restrain; reduce to obedience.
{{smaller block|<poem>The Minstrel fell!—but the foeman’s chain
Could not ''bring'' his proud soul ''under.''</poem>
{{right|''Moore'', Minstrel Boy.}}}}
'''To bring up.''' (''a'') To bear, convey, or lift upward. (''b'') In ''printing,'' to give the proper light and shade to, as a print of an engraving, by means of a suitable distribution of pressure in the press, produced by overlays; also, to equalize the pressure upon, as any part of a form on a press, by underlaying it with cardboard or paper. (''c'') In ''lithog.'', to make apparent; make visible, as a drawing or a greasy spot upon the stone. (''d'') To rear; nurture; care for during adolescence: used with reference to the needs of both the body and the mind.
{{smaller block|God by this tribulation calleth him, and biddeth him come home out of the countrey of sinne, that he was bred and ''brought vp'' so long in.
{{right|''Sir T. More'', Cumfort against Tribulation (1573), fol. 41.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
{{smaller block|I consider it the best part of an education to have been born and ''brought up'' in the country. {{right|''Alcott'', Tablets, p. 48.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The noble wish
To save all earnings to the uttermost,
And give his child a better ''bringing-up''
Than his had been. {{right|''Tennyson'', Enoch Arden.}}</poem>}}
{{smaller|(''e'') To introduce to notice or consideration: as, to ''bring up'' a subject in conversation. (''f'') To cause to advance near: as, to ''bring up'' forces, or the reserves.}}
{{smaller block|The troops from Corinth were ''brought up'' in time to repel the threatened movement without a battle. {{right|''U. S. Grant'', Personal Memoirs, I. 415.}}}}
{{smaller|(''g'') ''Naut.'', to stop (a ship’s headway) by letting go an anchor or by running her ashore. (''h'') To pull up (a horse); cause to stop: often with ''short'': as, he ''brought up'' his horse ''short'' (that is, caused it to stop suddenly); hence, figuratively, to stop suddenly in any career or course of action; bring before a magistrate; pull up.}}
{{smaller block|You were well aware that you were committing felony, and have probably felt tolerably sure at times that you would some day be ''brought up short''. {{right|''Trollope''.}}}}
{{smaller|'''To bring up the rear''', to move onward in the rear; form the rear portion.—'''To bring up with a round turn''' (''naut.''), to stop (the running of a rope) by taking a round turn on a belaying-pin or cavil; hence, figuratively, to stop the doing of anything suddenly but effectually. ='''Syn.''' ''Bring up'', ''Rear'', etc. See ''raise''.}}

'''bringer''' (bring′ėr), ''n.'' One who brings, in any sense of the verb.

'''brinish''' (brī′nish), ''a.'' [< ''brine''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ish''<sup>1</sup>.] Like brine; briny; salt or saltish: as, “her ''brinish'' tears,” ''Shak.'', 3 Hen. VI., iii. 1.

'''brinishness''' (brī′nish-nes), ''n.'' The quality of being brinish or saltish.
